{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3083","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3083","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3083\",\"parent_revision\":\"2a1dfdf06a5019dd4c4380c3277372e3917378475c89f7e358f95d10d0d65f7b\",\"root_revision\":\"2a1dfdf06a5019dd4c4380c3277372e3917378475c89f7e358f95d10d0d65f7b\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nFound while investigating a suspected (and ultimately false-alarm) data-loss incident during\\ngrooming of #3054 \u2014 the specific incident turned out to be a legitimate struck/corrected entry,\\nnot loss. But verifying it surfaced a real, separate, confirmed bug in the same neighborhood.\\n\\n`ContentWrite.reference` (`backlog_core/models.py:1273`) is typed as a `ContentRef`\\n(`backlog_core/models.py:1210-1216`: `kind`, `namespace`, `artifact_type`, `name` \u2014 no section\\nfield). `FileCache.queue_write()` (`backlog_core/file_cache.py`, lines ~141-149) coalesces the\\npending-mutation queue by comparing whole `ContentRef` objects:\\n\\n```python\\nfor entry in state.pending:\\n    if entry.write.reference == write.reference:\\n        if not inserted:\\n            pending.append(mutation)\\n            inserted = True\\n    else:\\n        pending.append(entry)\\n```\\n\\nTwo writes targeting *different sections* of the *same item* produce identical `ContentRef`s\\n(section identity isn't part of the ref at all), so they collide in this comparison. The\\nearlier pending mutation is discarded from the queue \u2014 not sent to GitHub, not preserved\\nanywhere \u2014 replaced by the later one.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] `queue_write`'s coalescing key includes section identity (or the section this write\\n      targets), not just the item-level `ContentRef`\\n- [ ] A reproduction test demonstrates two different-section writes to the same item both\\n      surviving in the pending queue and both reaching the backend\\n- [ ] Existing same-section coalescing behavior (intentional \u2014 a caller overwriting its own\\n      still-pending write to the same section) is preserved\\n</div>\\n\\n## Why This Is Masked In The Common Case\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`backlog_groom` queues the full serialized item state on every write, not a delta. If the same\\ncaller writes section A then section B in sequence, each write re-reads the item first, so the\\nsecond write's full-item snapshot already includes section A's change \u2014 no visible loss.\\n</div>\\n\\n## When It Actually Loses Data\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nTwo different callers (e.g. two parallel grooming-swarm agents) each read the item, then each\\nqueue a write to a *different* section, before either write reaches GitHub. The second queued\\nwrite's full-item snapshot was taken from a read that predates the first write. When the queue\\ncoalesces on `ContentRef` and keeps only the second entry, the first caller's section update is\\ngone \u2014 never reaches GitHub \u2014 with no error, no warning, and no trace beyond the discarded\\nin-memory queue entry.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproduction Status\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nNot yet reproduced end-to-end (the incident that surfaced this was a false alarm on the actual\\ndata-loss question). The code-level mechanism is confirmed by direct citation, not by triggering\\nthe failure live. A reproduction should: queue two writes to the same item's *different*\\nsections from what looks like two independent callers (two `queue_write` calls before either\\nsyncs), then inspect `state.pending` to confirm only one survives.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"06c8a146634269a392ef8ab1fc6c99237acaa7ffcb74c2d7b3da423f746bff3a\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIpkag\"}"}